# Line endings are pinned to LF, deliberately and load-bearingly.
#
# Taller compares generated files byte-for-byte against an in-memory render to
# detect tampering (spec 4.6). `atomic_write_text` always writes LF. With
# `core.autocrlf = true` — the default on Windows — git would hand those files
# back as CRLF on checkout, and the tamper check would report
# `constitution.resolved-snapshot-modified` at BLOCKER on a repository nobody
# had touched.
#
# Verified on this machine: a file committed as b"alpha\nbeta\n" returned from
# the worktree as b"alpha\r\nbeta\r\n".
#
# Every project Taller scaffolds gets this file too, for the same reason.

*               text=auto eol=lf

*.py            text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.cfg           text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf

# Generated files the tamper check reads. Never merged; regenerated instead
# (spec 4.6), so a merge driver of "ours" keeps a conflict from ever arising.
.taller/resolved.json               text eol=lf merge=ours
.taller/constitution/00-index.md    text eol=lf merge=ours

*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.svg           text eol=lf
